he slice's position is 0. So "from its current position to its limit" of the slice before reading is the whole remaining. So maybe they intend to assert that for each i from 0 to n-1, result.toLongArray()[i] == lb.get(original position + i). But after reading, the buffer's position changed; we can use \old(lb) to capture the buffer's state before method? However we cannot use observer methods on LongBuffer; but we can refer to the buffer's content via get? Not allowed because need to use only public observer methods provided in the test function. The test function is valueOf(LongBuffer lb). The only observer method on LongBuffer is get(int index) maybe? Actually LongBuffer has get() and get(int index). In JDK, LongBuffer has get() (relative) and get(int index) (absolute). The method uses relative get via get(words) which reads sequentially. For specification we can use lb.get(i) maybe. However we need to ensure we don't modify the buffer in spec; but using get(i) is fine as it's a read.

